<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">This treatise develops a fully categorical and sheaf-theoretic foundation for modular computation across heterogeneous linguistic domains. We trace the historical evolution from Descartes' coordinate geometry through Grothendieck's relative viewpoint to modern applied category theory in software systems. The central object is the </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">polyglot sheaf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} over a poset of repository substructures, equipped with descent conditions enforced by automated verification. We prove vanishing of higher ÄŒech cohomology under contractive gluing, establish equivalences with fibered categories and stacks, and demonstrate Kan extensions as semantic embeddings. Convergence of hermeneutic fixed points is quantified via spectral gaps in endofunctors on the category of repository states.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">abstract</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
