<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Historical Development of Categorical Modularity}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">history</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}[Descartes and Coordinate Geometry (1637)]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The quest for unified representation begins with Descartes' </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">La GÃ©omÃ©trie</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{descartes1637geometrie}, wherein algebraic equations are assigned to geometric loci. This establishes the first </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">gluing</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} of local charts (polynomial patches) into global curves, prefiguring sheaf conditions.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">history</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">history</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}[Riemann Surfaces and Analytic Continuation (1851)]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Riemann's introduction of multi-valued functions on branched covers </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{riemann1851grundlagen} necessitates consistent continuation across overlapping disksâ€”the analytic prototype of the sheaf gluing axiom.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">history</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">history</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}[Leray's Sheaf Theory (1945--1950)]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Jean Leray, developing cohomology during WWII imprisonment, defines sheaves as ``foncteurs'' assigning data to open sets with restriction compatibility </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{leray1945structure}. His ÄŒech cohomology computes global invariants from local data.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">history</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">history</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}[Cartan--Serre--Grothendieck Revolution (1950s)]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Grothendieck's </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">Tohoku</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} paper </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{grothendieck1957tohoku} reinterprets sheaves as representable functors, enabling abelian category methods. The relative viewpointâ€”sites, topoi, stacksâ€”emerges in SGA </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{sga4}.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">history</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">history</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}[Lawvere's Functorial Semantics (1963)]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">William Lawvere proposes categories as foundations for mathematics </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{lawvere1963functorial}, with functorial semantics assigning meaning via adjunctionsâ€”direct precursor to categorical type theory.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">history</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">history</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}[Applied Category Theory in Programming (1990s--2020s)]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Mac Lane's applied program manifests in functional programming (Haskell functors), dependent types (Agda, Idris), and verified compilation (CompCert). Spivak's applied category theory </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{spivak2014category} bridges theory and wiring diagrams.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">history</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
