<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">abstract</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">This comprehensive treatise establishes a fully categorical, sheaf-theoretic, and computationally grounded framework for modular software systems across heterogeneous linguistic domains. We trace the historical evolution from ancient conic sections through twentieth-century algebraic geometry and category theory to modern applied categorical software engineering. The central construction is the </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">polyglot sheaf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} over a poset of repository substructures equipped with a Grothendieck topology of admissible covers. We prove vanishing of higher ÄŒech cohomology under contractive gluing conditions, establish equivalences with fibered categories, stacks, and descent data, and provide algorithmic procedures for computing cohomology and Kan extensions. The hermeneutic dynamics of continuous integration are modeled as spectral endofunctors on the category of repository states, with exponential convergence rates. Extensive examples, case studies, and comparisons with existing module systems are provided. Appendices include formalizations in proof assistants and complexity analyses.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">abstract</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
